title: Deleting one's fork where it is drawn in its original's place under another's note deletes only the fork; under one's own note it removes the original too. Since 2026-11-15.
pid: bdd37c8c-f4c4-4d8d-bf5a-3f98c46cd7e9
body: |2-
  Every list names the original even where an overrider is drawn in its
  place ("It round-trips to the original on save" in
  'docs/sharing-model.org'). Under a parent the user does not own,
  'delete' on the drawn overrider now deletes it and leaves the original
  in the list: the save forks nothing, and the rerender draws the
  original there again ('deleted_overrider_keeps_its_original' in
  'server/from_text/local_fieldintent_collection/predicates.rs', asked
  by 'content_members' in 'traverse.rs', files of Terry's). Before, a
  deletion dropped the original from the list as a removal does, so in
  a view of another's note switched to one's own perspective, taking
  back one's reply to a message asked to fork the note: Ed's finding of
  2026-11-14, reproduced as he wrote it on 2026-11-15, even after
  deleting the reply first. Under a parent the user owns, and in a
  subscribee folder, the deletion still removes the original too (and
  in a subscriber hides it), as before: making the rule hold there as
  well was built, judged by a reviewer to change what those saves mean,
  and taken out. The fork's own children are not deleted with it.
  'docs/forks.org', "Taking back a fork", is the user's account. Open
  [[id:997e6a72-fbfa-497e-9df0-af575bb72b8d][the taking back of a fork as built: each choice, why, and the other way]]
  before changing any of it, or when Jeff answers one of those choices.
